      <section class="section story" id="story">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Built because I needed it.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              FoxLift started as a personal need and a weekend project. The available tools did not fit a modern CLI and CI workflow, so I started building one.
            </p>
            <p>
              Roughly four billion model tokens have gone into format research, oracle probes, corpus sweeps, failed hypotheses, code, and review.
            </p>
            <p>
              The standard is simple: recovered projects must rebuild and behave like the compiled input. Readable guesses are not enough.
            </p>
            <p>
              FoxLift is created and maintained by <a href="https://github.com/timo-bejan">timo-bejan</a>.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section process" id="process">
        <div class="shell process-grid">
          <div class="process-heading" data-reveal>
            <h2>What it does.</h2>
            <p>FoxLift follows a measured path from binary containers back to buildable VFP projects.</p>
          </div>

          <ol class="process-list" data-reveal>
            <li>
              <span>01</span>
              <div><h3>Read</h3><p>Find modules, forms, classes, reports, menus, and resources.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div><h3>Lift</h3><p>Decode measured bytecode, symbols, expressions, and control flow.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div><h3>Write</h3><p>Emit functionally equivalent VFP source and project artifacts.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div><h3>Verify</h3><p>Rebuild in VFP9 and compare canonical bytecode.</p></div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="receipts">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Round-trip, with receipts.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              The repo ships a small authored demo project. Compile it on VFP9, decompile it with the CLI, recompile the output: statement frames and symbol tables come back byte-identical. Exit codes, hashes, and per-section verdicts are committed alongside it.
            </p>
            <p>
              On the development corpus — one vendor's estate of real forms and class libraries — 10,514 of 10,514 method sections lift, none blocked, none silently dropped. On repositories never used to infer schemas, 95.1% of validation sections and 94.9% of held-out sections lift. Of the 286 sections whose emission changed in the latest round, 215 recompile byte-identical against the original bytecode on a real VFP9.
            </p>
            <p>
              Some things provably cannot come back: comments are never compiled, DECLARE parameter names are discarded, WAIT WINDOW clause order is canonicalised by the compiler, and some identifiers are blanked by the compiler itself. FoxLift proves what is unrecoverable and never invents it.
            </p>
            <a class="button" href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift/tree/main/demo">See the demo receipts</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="status">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Where we stand.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              Method code comes back today; whole rebuildable projects are the roadmap. In between, partial results are the contract, not a failure mode.
            </p>

            <dl class="stand-facts" aria-label="Project status">
              <div>
                <dt>Today</dt>
                <dd>Forms and class libraries (.scx/.vcx), compiled programs (.fxp), and module scans of .app/.exe decompile to per-module VFP source with symbols and per-section verdicts.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>In flight</dt>
                <dd>The rest of the compiled-program slice: DEFINE CLASS in .prg files, generated menu code, and code-page-faithful string literals.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Next</dt>
                <dd>Artifact reconstruction — writing back .scx, .vcx, .mnx and project glue so a recovered application rebuilds in VFP.</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <p>
              Hand FoxLift any binary and it tells the truth about it. A file that is not VFP is refused cleanly. A VFP binary always yields whatever is provable: every module found is decompiled, and any statement beyond the measured slice stays in the output as an annotated placeholder naming the exact blocker — never silently dropped. Exit 0 with a verified verdict means a 100% lift; exit 2 marks a partial.
            </p>
            <p>
              Concretely: a real production .exe from the development corpus currently comes back as 13 modules and roughly 10,900 lines of source — 159 of 160 sections lifted, and the one miss is labeled in place with the unmeasured SET variant that blocks it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Scope is Visual FoxPro 9 (SP2). Every claim above is measured against a real VFP9 compiler; nothing is guessed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="how">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>How we built it.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              A loop of overseers and a fleet of experimenters, with a real compiler as the referee.
            </p>

            <dl class="how-facts" aria-label="Campaign highlights">
              <div>
                <dt>3.7&#8202;B</dt>
                <dd>tokens through the worker fleet, at a 97.6% cache-hit rate</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>943</dt>
                <dd>mandates dispatched — all but 11 authored machine-to-machine</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>281</dt>
                <dd>worker lanes across 220 isolated worktrees</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>168&#8202;h</dt>
                <dd>of model time inside 92 wall-clock hours</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <p>
              Fleets of GLM-5.3-flash workers ran the experiments: each got one narrow task — one bytecode family, one parser region, one test file — in an isolated worktree, and stopped after one verified commit. <a href="https://claude.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a> and <a href="https://openai.com/codex/">Codex</a> ran the oversight loop: plan a wave, review every lane, integrate serially, re-run the gates, re-rank what blocks next, dispatch the next wave.
            </p>
            <p>
              Four days, measured: more than 55,000 tool calls across workers and overseers, with a watchdog shepherding crashed lanes back to work.
            </p>
            <p>
              No finding survived on model consensus. Every hypothesis was settled the same way: compile an authored probe on a real VFP9 and compare the bytecode.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

